prim:systemhood a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:comment "Signature role (D1, 2026-08-18): deliberately unassigned — Klir's name for an aspect of S (residing in R) — meta-classification, not a signature symbol. Not a gap; a verdict."@en ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "systemhood"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Klir's term, paired with thinghood."@en .

prim:thinghood a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:comment "Signature role (D1, 2026-08-18): deliberately unassigned — Klir's name for an aspect of S (residing in T) — meta-classification, not a signature symbol. Not a gap; a verdict."@en ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "thinghood"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Klir's term. Recorded as its own primitive rather than folded into `thing`, because whether it is distinct is a census question, not a modelling decision."@en .

prim:time-interval a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader role:constant ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "time interval"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Mobus's Δt: 'Finally, the last element in S is Δt, a time interval relevant to the level of the system of interest' (§4.3.3.6). The author's own WORD for the element; distinct from the census row prim:time, which records the notational Δt/dt invocations shared across authors."@en .

prim:transformation-rule a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader role:operation ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "transformation rule"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Mobus's T: 'T is the set of transformation rules for the subsystems in S' (§4.3.3.4). Hand-recovered after the model's wider span failed to locate — the passage is set in display-math wrappers the digitisation mangles."@en .

stance:bertalanffy-1968-ontic a atlas:StanceAssertion ;
    rdfs:comment """His own word — the chapter title names the position — which is why the
value is "perspectivist" rather than a characterisation in our words. The constraint is argued
evolutionarily, not asserted: categories that failed to correspond would have been selected
out, so the perspective is not free. The stance verbatim's "isomorphic ... with a certain
tolerance" applies to knowledge the same relation the shape categories apply to
formalisations; whether that is a connection or a pun is a mapping question, not asserted
here.

Attached to THIS entry, not 004: the passage is in the 1968 book, this entry's bearer. P3
read the chapter on 2026-08-03 (gst-1968-full-text.md:11478–11585). MDHC as of 2026-08-08:
model-drafted 2026-08-07, checked by Shingai the next day — including that the observer cell
stays empty rather than inferred."""@en ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-drafted from P3-stance-axes.md)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-07"^^xsd:date ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDHC ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "ch. 10, 'The Perspectivistic View'" ;
    atlas:stanceValue atlas:perspectivist ;
    atlas:verbatim "As for the connections of these stimuli, i.e., the categories of experience, they need not mirror the nexus of real events but must, with a certain tolerance allowed, be isomorphic to it" .

stance:klir-observer a atlas:StanceAssertion ;
    rdfs:comment """The iff does the work: describability is necessary AND sufficient, so the
act of distinguishing constitutes systemhood rather than selecting among systems already
there. Same page as the entry's verbatim.

THE UNIVERSALITY IS THE POINT, NOT AN OBJECTION. During the 2026-08-08 verifying read the
natural worry surfaced: can't ANY object be so described — two atoms at opposite ends of the
solar system, related by nothing but the observer and their instrument? Yes, and that is
mapping 001's Test 1 verdict (vacuously satisfiable, conceded by Klir himself). But a
criterion an observer can always satisfy is the signature of a constitutive act: if
describability suffices and describing is the observer's act, systemhood is conferred by
that act. The vacuity and the constitutivity are one fact read twice.

MDHC as of 2026-08-08: model-drafted 2026-08-07, checked by Shingai — the check that raised
and resolved the worry above."""@en ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-drafted from P3-stance-axes.md)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-07"^^xsd:date ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDHC ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "ch. 1, p. 5" ;
    atlas:stanceValue atlas:constitutive ;
    atlas:verbatim "an object is a system if and only if it can be described in a form that conforms to Eq. (1.1)" .

stance:klir-ontic a atlas:StanceAssertion ;
    rdfs:comment """First person plural, endorsing Gaines, not reporting him. READ PRECISELY:
the final clause permits distinctions drawn on real things, so this is constructivism about
SYSTEMHOOD, not anti-realism about things — the narrower claim his text supports, and why the
value is not plain "constructivist".

MDHC as of 2026-08-08: model-drafted from P3 on 2026-08-07, checked by Shingai the next
day. The passage was located during the P3 hunts and matches the registered transcription
source."""@en ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-drafted from P3-stance-axes.md)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-07"^^xsd:date ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDHC ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "§2.3, p. 21" ;
    atlas:stanceValue atlas:constructivist-about-systemhood ;
    atlas:verbatim "we should recognize that systems originate with us, human beings. We construct them by making appropriate distinctions, be they made in the real world by our perceptual capabilities or conceived in the world of ideas by our mental capabilities" .

cco:ont00000853 a owl:Class ;
