atlas:weakly-encompasses a skos:Concept ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:RelationScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "weakly encompasses"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "The source's form can redescribe the target; nothing need be preserved. Vacuously satisfiable by design — mapping 001 shows every catalogued definition passes, and that the constraints distinguishing the target are exactly what redescription is permitted to discard. A claim in this relation is deliberately weak evidence of anything."@en .

atlas:MappingClaim a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Mapping Claim"@en ;
    skos:definition "A reified relation between two entries, asserted by a mapping document. Carries its own evidence code and points at the argument; a claim of preservation owes a theorem name, a claim of loss owes a witness."@en .

atlas:StanceAssertion a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Stance Assertion"@en ;
    skos:definition "A reified reading of what an entry's definition is doing — one value on one axis, carried by the entry, graded by its own evidence code, licensed by its own verbatim."@en .

entry:klir-2001-eq-1-1 a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Klir (2001), Facets of Systems Science, eq. (1.1)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """VERIFIED: verbatim transcribed from the primary text, not paraphrased.

DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED: cco:ont00001808 (is about). Klir is an avowed
constructivist — on his account the relation R is defined by the modeller, not
discovered. Asserting what this definition is *about* would commit the catalogue
to a realist reading Klir does not hold. The neutrality is the point.

EXAMPLES NOTE: Klir gives no excluded example in Bunge's sense. His "a collection
of books is not a system, only a set" (p. 5, same paragraph as the included
examples) is the opening move of an argument whose conclusion is that organising
it *makes* it one, and on his own criterion — an object is a system iff it CAN BE
DESCRIBED in a form conforming to Eq. (1.1) — a bare collection is not refused but
merely not yet distinguished. Recording it as atlas:excludedExample would give it
the same form as Bunge's ontic refusal at Definition 1.1 and manufacture a symmetry
the two authors do not share; in a cases matrix it would read as agreement where
the finding is the opposite. The three included examples are grouped as Klir groups
them: his "etc." marks the variants as instances of a kind, not an enumeration of
cases. Source read 2026-08-03; see docs/proposals/P1-klir-examples.md and
docs/proposals/P3-stance-axes.md.

CENSUS NOTE: Klir's own gloss splits the definition's two slots into named
properties — thinghood in T, systemhood in R. Recorded as separate primitives
rather than folded into `thing` and `relation`, because whether they are distinct
primitives is a question the census should answer, not one the encoding should
prejudge.

ADJACENT MATERIAL, NOT YET ENTERED: Klir's self-assessment on the same page (the
definition is "too general and, consequently, of little pragmatic value" but
"encompasses all other, more specific definitions of systems") is a claim ABOUT
this definition's relation to others. It belongs in the mapping layer, not in
this entry.

NEXT TIER: the dependency quiver (tier 1) for this definition already exists in
Lean as ShapeKlir in systems-science-foundations. The two encodings must be
checked against each other before either is cited."""@en ;
    atlas:admits case:klir-books-ordered,
        case:klir-books-partitioned,
        case:klir-population-by-birth ;
    atlas:displayContext "where S, T, R denote, respectively, a system, a set of things distinguished within S, and a relation (or, possibly, a set of relations) defined on T." ;
    atlas:displayForm "S = (T, R)" ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-01"^^xsd:date ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:HVP ;
    atlas:floorDependency prim:relation ;
    atlas:floorPosition prim:thing ;
    atlas:formalisedAs "Systems/Category/ShapeKlir.lean#KlirShape" ;
    atlas:hasStance stance:klir-observer,
        stance:klir-ontic ;
    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:relation,
        prim:set,
        prim:systemhood,
        prim:thing,
        prim:thinghood ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "ch. 1, eq. (1.1), p. 5" ;
    atlas:statedIn bearer:klir-2001-facets ;
    atlas:verbatim """S = (T, R),   (1.1)

where S, T, R denote, respectively, a system, a set of things distinguished within S, and a relation (or, possibly, a set of relations) defined on T. Clearly, the thinghood and systemhood properties of S reside in T and R, respectively.""" .

prim:environment a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader role:sort ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "environment"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Bunge's E. Things outside the composition that act on, or are acted on by, components. Mobus's revised tuple (entry 007) also carries an E — absent from his published 2022 seven-tuple, which is why the row lights up only on the revisions entry: the difference records the revision."@en .

prim:time a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader role:sort ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "time"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Bunge's triple is indexed at a time t. Klir's is not. A candidate point of divergence. In Bertalanffy's eq. (3.1) the invocation is notational — the t of dQᵢ/dt — rather than the word. Mobus's Δt (Eq. 4.1) is likewise notational at the definition; the word arrives only in the tuple's elaboration."@en .

atlas:EvidenceCode a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence Code"@en ;
    skos:definition "How an encoding was established. Modelled on the Gene Ontology's evidence codes: every assertion carries a record of its provenance strength, and unverified assertions remain visible and filterable rather than silently equal to verified ones."@en .

atlas:SignatureRoleScheme a skos:ConceptScheme ;
    rdfs:label "Signature roles"@en ;
    skos:definition "Model theory's signature vocabulary, fixed prior to and independent of the corpus. What slot a primitive's word fills in a formal statement — checkable against the formula, unlike meaning."@en .

prim:element a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader role:sort ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "element"@en .

role:operation a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A function symbol: maps arguments to a value (transfer functions, Bunge's composition-at-a-time)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:SignatureRoleScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "operation"@en .

skos:ConceptScheme a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Concept Scheme"@en ;
    skos:definition "A set of concepts, optionally including statements about semantic relationships between those concepts."@en .

atlas:Author a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Author"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """NO DEFINITIONAL CONTENT ATTACHES HERE. The same discipline that scopes
stance (below) scopes everything else: a person may define "system" differently in different
works — the catalogue's first author does — so verbatims, primitives, cases, stances and
evidence codes all attach to the ENTRY or the BEARER, never to the author. An author node that
accumulated a "position" would manufacture a consistency the corpus is here to test. What an
author node licenses is exactly one derived fact: which entries share an author, and therefore
where a revision arc exists to be examined entry by entry.

Declared standalone rather than subclassed into the CCO agent hierarchy, on the
atlas:statedIn precedent: declared plainly now rather than asserted wrongly."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A person to whom the catalogue's bearers attribute authorship. Identity
only: a name that several works share, so that "Bertalanffy 1968" and "Bertalanffy 1972" can be
recognised as one person revising rather than two people agreeing."""@en .

prim:set a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:comment "Signature role (D1, 2026-08-18): deliberately unassigned — ambient set theory — the metalanguage the definitions are written in, not a symbol of any signature. Not a gap; a verdict."@en ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "set"@en .

prim:thing a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader role:sort ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "thing"@en .

prim:relation a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader role:relation ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:PrimitiveScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "relation"@en .

role:relation a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A relation symbol, including graphs read as edge relations and orderings."@en ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:SignatureRoleScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "relation"@en .

cco:ont00000253 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Information Bearing Entity"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf obo:BFO_0000030 ;
    skos:definition "An Object upon which an Information Content Entity generically depends."@en .

atlas:MDHC a atlas:EvidenceCode ;
    rdfs:label "model-drafted, human-checked"@en ;
    skos:definition "A model produced the encoding; a human checked it against the verbatim text."@en .

atlas:FormalSystemDefinition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Formal System Definition"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Inclusion criteria are stated in the spec, §5. Borderline cases are adjudicated there, not here."@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cco:ont00000853 ;
    skos:definition """A Descriptive Information Content Entity that defines the
term "system" formally: it introduces named components with types or sorts, and
fixes at least one relation, function, or constraint among them, in symbols or in
prose precise enough to be transcribed to symbols without adding content."""@en .

role:sort a skos:Concept ;
    skos:definition "A carrier kind: what the definition quantifies over or holds as an entity slot."@en ;
    skos:inScheme atlas:SignatureRoleScheme ;
    skos:prefLabel "sort"@en .

atlas:Example a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Example"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """WHY THIS IS A CLASS. Examples were literal strings on the entry until
2026-08-03, which made four things impossible at once: an example could carry no evidence code
of its own, so one entry-level code graded a verbatim, five primitives and three examples
alike; no source location, though Bunge's examples sit outside the location his entry claims;
no separation of the case from the author's reason for it, concatenated with an em dash; and
no identity, so two authors ruling on the same object could not be matched. See
docs/proposals/P4-evidence-scope.md."""@en ;
    skos:definition """A case an author rules on: something they offer as a system, or refuse as
one. Reified rather than recorded as a string on the entry, because an example is a thing
several claims are made about -- where it appears in the source, how the encoding was
established, and which shared case it instantiates."""@en .

atlas:HVP a atlas:EvidenceCode ;
    rdfs:label "human-verified against primary text"@en ;
    skos:definition "A human read the primary source and confirmed the verbatim text and the encoding against it."@en .

atlas:PrimitiveScheme a skos:ConceptScheme ;
    rdfs:label "Systems-definition primitive vocabulary"@en ;
    skos:definition "Controlled vocabulary of terms used as primitives in formal definitions of system. Deliberately flat and deliberately not an ontology of the world."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """LEXICAL, NOT SEMANTIC. A primitive here records that an author uses a WORD as
a primitive. Two entries linked to prim:thing are not thereby claimed to mean the same
thing by it — Klir's "thing" is unrestricted, Bunge's ranges over concrete entities in
Theta, and whether those are one primitive or two is a census question this vocabulary
must not prejudge.

The consequence: recurrence counts computed over this scheme are a FIRST-PASS LEXICAL
measure. Semantic co-reference is a mapping-layer claim and owes evidence like any
other. Minting a separate primitive per author would make recurrence trivially zero;
merging them by hand would make it trivially high. Lexical recurrence is the honest
starting point, and its limits are stated here rather than discovered later."""@en .

skos:Concept a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Concept"@en ;
    skos:definition "An idea or notion; a unit of thought."@en .

